\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E004 | Pipeline-run comparison data validation | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E005 | Document the run comparison workflow | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9015-E001<br/>Dashboard comparison view] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9015-E003<br/>Registry comparison entry]\n    E002[RHAISTRAT-9015-E002<br/>Model version run lineage] --> E003\n    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9015-E005<br/>Comparison docs]\n    E002 --> E005\n    E003 --> E005\n    E004[RHAISTRAT-9015-E004<br/>Pipeline-run data validation] --> E005\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The registry entry point navigates to the comparison surface; it is a consumer of the comparison view, not a second builder of it. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The registry comparison entry renders the run reference stored on model versions; it cannot be built before that data exists. |\n| E001 → E005, E002 → E005, E003 → E005, E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | Dashboard comparison and registry lineage are independent surfaces in different repos; they run in parallel and meet in E003. |\n| No edge E004 → E001 | Rule 4 / Step 3 decision rule | Data Science Pipelines is passive (no code changes; per-run data already surfaced via the KFP v2 API per architecture context). The validation does not change which epics exist or what they build — a found gap is fix-and-retry remediation, and the strategy declares no open questions — so it runs in parallel with the Dashboard work rather than gating it. |\n| No edge E001 → E002 (serialization) | Priority ordering | Forcing the P0 comparison view to wait on P1 registry lineage (or folding the registry entry point into the P0 epic, which would make it depend on P1 work) would create a priority inversion; the registry entry is a separate logical change (E003). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Side-by-side comparison of two or more runs in the Dashboard. Pipeline-run data availability is cross-checked by E004. |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E002, E003 | Split across components per Rule 1: E002 stores the run reference on model versions (Model Registry); E003 opens the comparison from the registry UI (Dashboard). |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 | **Bundled (consolidated, never split):** the diff view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run data — the strategy itself notes overlapping wording kept from the two customer council sources. One epic covers both HLR-1 and HLR-3 at the higher priority (P0). Splitting would produce two epics building the same UI surface plus an artificial serialization edge between them; on a shared surface the P0/P1 pair is redundant wording, not a deferral signal. |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n\nAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted\n  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and\n  the architecture context; per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n  HLR wording. Owners should confirm the inferred team assignments.\n- **Documentation epic ownership assumed:** no architecture-context file exists\n  for the Documentation component and the strategy names no docs team; E005 is\n  assigned to RHOAI Dashboard (the primary surface team). Re-assign if a\n  dedicated docs team owns this content.\n- **Component span on E001:** the comparison view is implemented in the\n  Dashboard but consumes run data for pipeline runs from Data Science\n  Pipelines. `component` is set to the single most-relevant canonical name\n  (`Dashboard`); the Data Science Pipelines involvement is carried by E004\n  rather than combined into the Dashboard component value.\n- **Coordination risk (not a dependency):** E004 validates pipeline-run\n  parameter/metric exposure in parallel with E001. If a gap is found, the\n  remediation lands as follow-up work in the Dashboard comparison (or Data\n  Science Pipelines if exposure is missing); this is fix-and-retry per the\n  strategy's \"Open Questions: None\", not a gate.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — there are no external dependencies with uncertain timing; all\nwork is in-platform and the P0 comparison view (E001) delivers independent\nuser value without the P1 lineage work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":27711,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b835ba31bc4f7427","agent_id":"a06bb193319fffa43","session_id":"44d00f4c-d13b-4638-bc59-4a21076b9c90","uuid":"b862ccfb-e2e5-4576-a5fc-b996d78b8f26","timestamp":"2026-10-23T18:48:24.163Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9015"}
