{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaaed35f25f004c0","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9015\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-9015 — Experiment comparison for training and pipeline runs\n10\t\n11\tStrategy RHAISTRAT-9015 adds run comparison to the platform: users select two or\n12\tmore training or pipeline runs and view parameters and metrics side by side\n13\t(including a diff highlighting of what changed), registered model versions link\n14\tback to their producing run so a comparison can be opened from the Model\n15\tRegistry, and the workflow is documented.\n16\t\n17\t**Triage outcome:** full decomposition. The strategy is not below-threshold —\n18\tit spans three components (Dashboard, Model Registry, Data Science Pipelines)\n19\tand multiple architecturally distinct work streams (frontend comparison UI,\n20\tregistry lineage metadata, cross-team validation, documentation). It is not\n21\tdocumentation-only. The strategy declares no open questions, risks, or\n22\tconditional ADRs, so no Investigation epics are needed.\n23\t\n24\t**Team attribution note:** the strategy omits the Impacted Teams table; owner\n25\tteams are inferred from the architecture-context owning-team fields (Dashboard\n26\t→ RHOAI Dashboard, Model Registry → Model Registry, Data Science Pipelines →\n27\tRHOAI Pipelines). See Health Warnings.\n28\t\n29\t## Epic List\n30\t\n31\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n32\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n33\t| RHAISTRAT-9015-E001 | Dashboard run comparison view (side-by-side and diff) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n34\t| RHAISTRAT-9015-E002 | Model version run lineage in the Model Registry | Implementation | Model Registry | P1 |\n35\t| RHAISTRAT-9015-E003 | Open run comparison from the Model Registry UI | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-9015-E004 | Pipeline-run comparison data validation | Implementation | RHOAI Pipelines | P1 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-9015-E005 | Document the run comparison workflow | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n38\t\n39\t## Dependency DAG\n40\t\n41\t```mermaid\n42\tgraph TD\n43\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9015-E001<br/>Dashboard comparison view] --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9015-E003<br/>Registry comparison entry]\n44\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9015-E002<br/>Model version run lineage] --> E003\n45\t    E001 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9015-E005<br/>Comparison docs]\n46\t    E002 --> E005\n47\t    E003 --> E005\n48\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9015-E004<br/>Pipeline-run data validation] --> E005\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\t## DAG Justification\n52\t\n53\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n54\t|------|------|-----------|\n55\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | The registry entry point navigates to the comparison surface; it is a consumer of the comparison view, not a second builder of it. |\n56\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The registry comparison entry renders the run reference stored on model versions; it cannot be built before that data exists. |\n57\t| E001 → E005, E002 → E005, E003 → E005, E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; it is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n58\t| No edge E001 ↔ E002 | Rule 14 (different repos, no shared artifacts) | Dashboard comparison and registry lineage are independent surfaces in different repos; they run in parallel and meet in E003. |\n59\t| No edge E004 → E001 | Rule 4 / Step 3 decision rule | Data Science Pipelines is passive (no code changes; per-run data already surfaced via the KFP v2 API per architecture context). The validation does not change which epics exist or what they build — a found gap is fix-and-retry remediation, and the strategy declares no open questions — so it runs in parallel with the Dashboard work rather than gating it. |\n60\t| No edge E001 → E002 (serialization) | Priority ordering | Forcing the P0 comparison view to wait on P1 registry lineage (or folding the registry entry point into the P0 epic, which would make it depend on P1 work) would create a priority inversion; the registry entry is a separate logical change (E003). |\n61\t\n62\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n63\t\n64\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n65\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n66\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | Side-by-side comparison of two or more runs in the Dashboard. Pipeline-run data availability is cross-checked by E004. |\n67\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E002, E003 | Split across components per Rule 1: E002 stores the run reference on model versions (Model Registry); E003 opens the comparison from the registry UI (Dashboard). |\n68\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 | **Bundled (consolidated, never split):** the diff view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run data — the strategy itself notes overlapping wording kept from the two customer council sources. One epic covers both HLR-1 and HLR-3 at the higher priority (P0). Splitting would produce two epics building the same UI surface plus an artificial serialization edge between them; on a shared surface the P0/P1 pair is redundant wording, not a deferral signal. |\n69\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception. |\n70\t\n71\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n72\t\n73\t## Health Warnings\n74\t\n75\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted\n76\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n77\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Decomposition proceeded from the HLRs and\n78\t  the architecture context; per-epic acceptance criteria were derived from the\n79\t  HLR wording. Owners should confirm the inferred team assignments.\n80\t- **Documentation