dated from two\n  customer councils. This is the mirror case done correctly — bundling is right for a\n  shared surface, the analysis is on record where the mapping is read, and E001's HLR\n  Traceability section repeats it. No unanalyzed priority collapse.\n- HLR-4 (P2) → E005 (docs-authoring), with the docs-authoring priority exception applied\n  correctly (strategy `priority: Major` → P1).\n\nPriority inheritance: E001 (the only P0 epic) has no dependencies. E004 traces to HLR-1 at\nP1 but blocks no P0 work — its only outgoing edge is to the docs epic — and the matrix row\nexplicitly records why validation is schedulable at P1 in parallel rather than inheriting\nP0. E002/E003 are P1 and block no P0 work. No inheritance violations.\n\n### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n\nDiagram/frontmatter cross-check: every mermaid edge (E001→E003, E002→E003, E001→E005,\nE002→E005, E003→E005, E004→E005) has a matching entry in the target epic's frontmatter\n`dependencies`, and vice versa. Arrows are drawn dependency→dependent per the `graph TD`\nconvention. No cycles, no references to nonexistent epics, no branch files.\n\nEdge justification:\n- E001→E003 and E002→E003 are justified: E003 is a consumer of both the comparison view\n  (E001) and the run reference stored/exposed by E002 — the E002→E003 edge is a genuine\n  data dependency (E003 renders data E003 does not produce).\n- All→E005 are Rule 11 docs-last edges, which are valid and do not trigger priority\n  inheritance. The validator confirms docs-last coverage.\n- Parallelism: E001 and E002 run in parallel (different repos/components, no shared\n  artifacts) and meet in E003 — correct. E004 runs parallel to E001 by explicit reasoned\n  decision: the validation outcome does not change which epics exist (fix-and-retry\n  remediation, strategy declares no open questions), and E001's acceptance criterion\n  (\"comparison works for pipeline runs using the run data already surfaced\") is the\n  correct AC-gate shape rather than a dependencies edge. This is not a missing edge.\n- No konflux-onboarding, license-validation, or repo-onboarding epics exist, so the\n  Rules 7/8/10 mandated-parallel pairings do not apply.\n\nCross-check completeness: no epic consumes a sibling-produced schema, API, or artifact\nwithout an edge — E003's consumption of E002's REST API exposure and E001's view are both\nedged; E004 consumes nothing from siblings; E005 depends on everything it documents.\n\nCritical path length 3 (E001→E003→E005), within the M-strategy heuristic of 3-4 for a\n3-component strategy.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEach epic is a single component/team tuple: (Dashboard, RHOAI Dashboard) ×2, (Model\nRegistry, Model Registry), (Data Science Pipelines, RHOAI Pipelines), (Documentation,\nRHOAI Dashboard). Team attribution is inferred from architecture-context owning-team fields\nand flagged in Health Warnings for owner confirmation — reasonable given the strategy omits\nthe Impacted Teams table.\n\n- E001 and E003 share a tuple but are separate logical changes (comparison surface vs.\n  registry entry point that also depends on E002's data) — a legitimate split, not a\n  boundary violation.\n- No scope duplication: E003 consumes the comparison view rather than rebuilding it; the\n  two presentation modes of the comparison surface (side-by-side and diff) are correctly\n  kept in ONE epic (E001) rather than split across a P0/P1 pair.\n- No work-product type mixing: the docs epic (E005) is separate from all code epics.\n- No oversized epics; E001 is one cohesive surface (selection + side-by-side + diff over\n  the same data), well within the ~2-week guideline.\n- The Dashboard/Data Science Pipelines span on E001 is handled correctly: `component` is\n  the single canonical name, and the pipelines involvement is carried by E004 with an\n  explicit Health Warning.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (comparison UI, API\nsurface + stored run reference, registry entry point, validation report, published docs).\nNo Investigation epics — justified: the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and the\nsummary reasons about why none are needed.\n\nE004 is the one judgment call and it is handled correctly: it validates an assumption, but\nthe decomposition explicitly applies the type test — the validation outcome does not change\nwhich downstream epics exist or what they do (a found gap is fix-and-retry remediation\ninput), so an Investigation with gating structure would be wrong; Implementation producing\na validation-report artifact is the rule-compliant shape, and team ownership (RHOAI\nPipelines) justifies it being its own epic rather than an AC folded into E001.\n\nAll `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are null and no epic lists an Investigation in\n`dependencies`, so the gating-metadata checks are trivially satisfied.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nAll five epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (not `investigation_signals`)\n— type-appropriate. All five ai-signals files exist; frontmatter values match the rationale\ntables exactly; `ai_implementability`/`ai_implementability_score` are pipeline-computed and\nnot re-derived.\n\nCalibration spot-checks:\n- `architecture_claims: 1` on all epics, each citing specific architecture-context files —\n  verified against `.context`: dashboard.md (single-pane console, pipelines surfaced),\n  model-registry.md (custom properties on ModelVersion as the metadata extension point,\n  Dashboard hosts the registry UI), data-science-pipelines.md (KFP v2 REST API, runs\n  surfaced in the Dashboard). The citations are accurate, including E003's use of\n  model-registry.md for the Dashboard-hosted registry UI.\n- `open_questions: 1` is consistent — the strategy explicitly declares \"Open Questions:\n  None\". E004's rationale correctly distinguishes the assumption it validates (its own\n  deliverable, fix-and-retry failure mode